A lot of people put sugar in their coffee because the coffee needs it. Cheap, stale or over-extracted coffee is bitter and flat, and sugar covers that up. Good coffee, brewed well, doesn't need much help. When I find beans I like, I don't reach for sugar at all.

This is part one of [Low-Carb Coffee & Milk](/coffee/guides/low-carb-coffee/).

## Pick beans that taste sweet

Coffee doesn't have sugar in it once it's brewed, but some coffees taste a lot sweeter than others. That sweetness comes from the beans and the roast, and you can look for it on the bag.

- **Tasting notes** like caramel, brown sugar, milk chocolate, honey, toffee, stone fruit or berries usually mean a sweeter-tasting cup. There's more on reading them in [Reading a Specialty Coffee Bag](/coffee/guides/reading-a-coffee-bag/).
- **Natural and honey processed** coffees tend to taste sweeter and fruitier than washed ones. The seed spends longer with the fruit, and it shows. See [Coffee Processing Methods](/coffee/guides/coffee-processing-methods/).
- **Origins** like Brazil, Colombia and Guatemala often make round, chocolatey, sweet coffee that's easy to drink black. A natural Ethiopian can taste like blueberries. See [Coffee Origins](/coffee/guides/coffee-origins/).

## Pick the right roast

Roast level makes a big difference, and the best one for drinking black depends on what's been putting you off.

- **If black coffee tastes bitter to you,** try a **medium roast**. It has the most caramel and chocolate sweetness, and less of the roasty bitterness of a dark roast.
- **If you like bright, fruity flavors,** try a **light roast**. Brewed well, a good light roast can taste like fruit juice or tea.
- **Dark roasts** are the hardest to drink black, since they lean smoky and bittersweet. They're made to stand up to milk.

There's more in [Coffee Roast Levels](/coffee/guides/coffee-roast-levels/).

## Brew it so it isn't bitter

Even great beans taste bad when they're brewed badly. A few things from [Brewing Basics](/coffee/guides/brewing-basics/) matter most here:

- **Use fresh beans and grind them right before brewing.** Stale coffee tastes flat and papery. A [hand grinder](/coffee/reviews/hand-coffee-grinders/) was the best upgrade I made.
- **Don't over-extract.** If it tastes bitter and dry, grind coarser, use slightly cooler water, or brew a little shorter. See [Dialing In by Taste](/coffee/guides/dialing-in-coffee-by-taste/).
- **Try a lighter ratio.** Strong coffee is harder to drink black. Going from 15:1 to 17:1 makes a softer, easier cup.
- **Use a paper filter.** [Pour-over](/coffee/guides/pour-over-coffee/) and drip make a cleaner, lighter cup than a French press, which is easier to start with.
- **Don't let it sit on a hot plate.** Coffee that's been cooking for an hour gets bitter fast.

## Easing off sugar

If you're used to sweet coffee, going straight to black can be a shock. Easing into it works better for a lot of people:

- **Cut back a little at a time.** Use a bit less sugar each week. Your taste adjusts more than you'd expect.
- **Switch to a zero-carb sweetener** while you get used to it. I use [monk fruit](/ingrts/monk-fruit-sweetener/) drops in coffee, and there's more on that in my [pantry staples](/blog/new-pantry-staples/) post.
- **Add flavor without sugar.** A sprinkle of cinnamon in the grounds before brewing, or a drop of vanilla extract in the cup.
- **A tiny pinch of salt** in the grounds or the cup takes the edge off bitterness. Really, a tiny pinch.
- **Add an unsweetened milk** instead of a sweetened creamer. There's a chart of the options in [Unsweetened Milks and Creamers](/coffee/guides/unsweetened-milks-and-creamers/).

## Let it cool a bit

Coffee tastes different as it cools. Very hot coffee mostly tastes bitter and roasty, and a lot of the sweetness and fruit comes out as it drops to a warm, drinkable temperature. Give it a few minutes, and keep tasting as you drink it.
